#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267 :topic/difficulty 0.6911657375018757 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428062 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1047->148. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "3afe28a0-5051-52a1-a085-a06727c301b8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096456 :question/id #uuid "201a713a-83ca-5e31-9a4f-257795d7eb1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4690" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=3 \\\\ y+z &=2 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(2)=2 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = 2-2 = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $z=2$ and $y=0$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(0)+(2)=3\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx = 3-2 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x = 1,$ $y=0,$ and $z = 2.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 1,0,2 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8f8937b-de15-5d4c-aa77-1d45595fc7af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096457 :question/id #uuid "cc8ce97a-3bda-506f-b9d2-98ac7524583a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4691" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $x_3=5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(5)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-5=-7.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 5$ and $x_2 = -7$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx_1+(-7)-(5) = 1\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = 1 + 7 + 5 = 13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 13,$ $x_2=-7,$ and $x_3 = 5.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 13,-7,5 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de05090a-2461-514a-971d-b83c7cea124e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096671 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096458 :question/id #uuid "7b44089c-8ca7-57e5-b390-7900647e3884" :question/math-academy-id "e-4692" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n3x_3=3 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\tx_3=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(1)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-1=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 1$ and $x_2 = -3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x_1+(-3)-(1) = 0\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (3 + 1) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 2,$ $x_2=-3,$ and $x_3 = 1.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 2,-3,1 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e51c9a-6bce-5ea6-a9fe-febb09f8754d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Given an Augmented Matrix Containing Non-Unit Leading Entries"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1047 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049445}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Back Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527 :topic/difficulty 0.2853007808149399 :topic/id #uuid "508ac00e-0867-53e8-a192-d981ff4bbdc2" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161965 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161757 :question/id #uuid "70991204-370a-5804-a04e-7e664db052de" :question/math-academy-id "e-8048" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & {\\color{red}0} & -3 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ -4 & {\\color{red}0} & -5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{red}0} & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- $R_1 := \\ldots$\n\n- $R_3:=R_3-3R_2$\n\n- $R_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below $a_{22}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}: &={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_1 := R_1 - 2R_2\\quad$ and $\\quad R_4:=R_4+2R_2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161966 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161758 :question/id #uuid "cbf8e85c-ffa6-55b9-a087-2a5272f10f25" :question/math-academy-id "e-8049" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & {\\color{blue}-3} & -1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & {\\color{blue}-2} & -2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & 4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & {\\color{blue}-3} & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & {\\color{blue}-3} & 11 & 0 \\\\ -3 & {\\color{blue}-2} & 6 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ -23 & {\\color{blue}-3} & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\n- $C_1:=\\ldots$\n\n- $C_3:= C_3 - 4C_2$\n\n- $C_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{32}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}: &={C}_{1}+5{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -23 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}-4{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -3 & 11 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -23 & -3 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}: &={C}_{4}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -3 & 11 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -23 & -3 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+5C_2\\quad$ and $\\quad C_4:=C_4-2C_2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "958cdcf3-aaba-5275-85f8-9531f10b3d0e" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161967 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161759 :question/id #uuid "399b7598-c793-5032-9f33-ac02c7b85fe1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8050" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}4} & {\\color{blue}-7} \\\\ 2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}4} & {\\color{blue}-7} \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & -6 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & 10 & -20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_1,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below $a_{11}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_2:=R_2-2R_1\\quad$ and $\\quad R_3:=R_3+3R_1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161760 :question/id #uuid "f9912357-1d1d-5413-917e-257dc5dfc0fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8051" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} \\\\ 8 & 3 & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ 9 & 7 & {\\color{blue}-2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} \\\\ -16 & -17 & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ -3 & -3 & {\\color{blue}-2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_3,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of $a_{13}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}: &={C}_{1}+6{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}+5{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & -17 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+6C_3\\quad$ and $\\quad C_2:=C_2+5C_3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3029 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050576}] :topic/title "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination"}]}
